## Hands-on music, rhythm and culture

Our Drum Sing Dance programs are packed with interactive, educational and multicultural experiences. In addition, a wonderful variety of African drums and percussion means every child can make music.

First, children explore their own sounds. Then they learn fun musical patterns and discover rhythm, tempo, dynamics and sound.

### A journey through African music and culture

Our kindergarten incursions also take children on a journey through African music and culture. Along the way, they enjoy stories, singing, dancing and activities that explore the body’s natural rhythm. As a result, they discover the joy of making music together with their friends.

What’s more, there are five different incursions to choose from, so you can pick the one that suits your children best.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% section tone="grey" %}
{% columns layout="equal" align="center" %}
{% column %}
*For educators*

## Supporting the EYLF through music, movement & play

Our African Drum Sing Dance kindergarten incursions align with the [Early Years Learning Framework (EYLF)](https://www.acecqa.gov.au/nqf/national-law-regulations/approved-learning-frameworks): Belonging, Being and Becoming. Through music, movement, singing and shared participation, children explore, discover and learn in a fun and supportive environment.

In other words, they give educators a meaningful way to extend children’s learning through an experience that is joyful, inclusive and engaging.

### Belonging & connection

As children share rhythms, take turns and respond to one another, they build relationships and social skills.

### Let’s get physical!

Drumming, singing and dancing also support physical wellbeing, coordination, body awareness and confidence.

### Cultural learning

Importantly, the program introduces West African music and dance, so children develop respect for different traditions.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
![Smiling young girl playing a djembe drum outdoors during an early childhood drumming incursion](/media/2019/02/Wildlings-1389-e1550208803338.jpg)
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}
{% /section %}

*Learning through play*

## What children learn in our kindergarten incursions

Our programs offer young children a playful way to explore music, movement, culture and connection. Every activity also supports important skills across the early childhood curriculum.

{% columns layout="equal" align="top" %}
{% column %}
### Musical development

Children explore drumming, singing, call-and-response songs and percussion. As a result, they discover rhythmic patterns, develop a sense of beat and learn to listen and respond.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
### Physical development

Through African dance and movement games, children also develop coordination, balance, gross motor skills and spatial awareness.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
### Hands-on participation

Every child has an instrument, so everyone is actively involved. Children experiment with sounds, take turns and respond to musical cues.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% columns layout="equal" align="top" %}
{% column %}
### Cognitive development

For example, children practise counting, recognising patterns, remembering sequences and following instructions. Singing and storytelling also introduce new words.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
### Social & emotional growth

Making music together means children practise sharing, taking turns and listening to others. In addition, they celebrate each other’s participation.
{% /column %}

{% column %}
### Cultural learning

Children discover how music and movement are part of West African traditions and celebrations, so they grow curious about cultural diversity.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

### Creative expression

There is no single right way to join in. Instead, children make their own sounds and movements, which nurtures imagination and confidence.

### Learning through play, the way young children learn best

Children take part at their own pace. For example, some confidently lead a rhythm, while others dance along, explore an instrument or simply listen. Either way, every child shares in the joy of making music together.

{% section tone="dark" %}
## What happens during our kindergarten incursions?

Get ready for a lively, hands-on musical adventure! An experienced facilitator leads each session, so it’s filled with drumming, singing, dancing, storytelling and plenty of laughter.

- **African drumming:** Children meet the djembe and a collection of unique African percussion. Then they learn simple rhythms, keep a beat and hear how their rhythms come together as an ensemble.
- **Body percussion:** Who needs an instrument when you have your own body? So children clap, tap, stamp and pat to create rhythm, which builds listening and coordination.
- **Singing & call-and-response:** Through lively African songs and chants, children listen, repeat musical phrases and respond to cues. As a result, they build language, memory and listening skills.
- **African dance & movement:** Children also learn fun, accessible African-inspired dance moves. Through movement games, they develop coordination and balance while expressing themselves.
- **Stories & cultural discovery:** Along the way, children hear stories and learn where the instruments come from, as well as the materials and traditional techniques used to make them.
- **A shared musical experience:** Throughout the workshop, children have a go, take turns and follow musical cues. Finally, they enjoy playing together as one group.
{% /section %}

{% columns layout="equal" align="center" %}
{% column %}
*Workshop details*

## Kindergarten incursion details at a glance

- **Duration:** 30 to 45 minutes, depending on age group and centre needs
- **Ages:** Babies to pre-prep
- **Instruments:** African djembes and unique percussion, all supplied
- **Participation:** Every child plays an instrument and joins in
- **Activities:** Drumming, body percussion, singing, call-and-response, dance and storytelling
- **Experience:** None needed, because activities suit each child’s age and stage

Our incursions are inclusive, engaging and developmentally appropriate. As a result, children join in their own way while sharing the excitement of a group musical experience.

